<Layout title="How it works" description="How the DOS game became a C# program: translation, emulated machine, project layout and tests.">
  <h1>How it works</h1>
  <p class="lead">From a 16-bit DOS program to a .NET application, one function at a time.</p>

  <h2>From CIV.EXE to C#</h2>
  <p>
    Every function of the original <code>CIV.EXE</code> was translated into a C# method. At first the translation
    followed the machine code closely: registers, the stack and memory addresses, all kept as they were. Step by step
    that code was rewritten into ordinary C#, with names, parameters and return values, while tests checked that the
    game still behaved exactly like the first translation.
  </p>
  <p>
    Some places still read the game's memory by address. That memory is an emulated 8086 machine: the game's data,
    the saved game and the tables of the original live there, and the game reads them the way the original did.
  </p>

  <h2>Pictures, sound and data</h2>
  <p>
    Pictures, palettes, fonts and texts come from the files of your copy of the game. The data of the program itself
    is unpacked from the original <code>CIV.EXE</code> at startup. Sound drivers of the original run in a small machine
    code interpreter, which drives an emulated AdLib synthesizer or translates Roland MT-32 music to General MIDI.
  </p>

  <h2>Projects</h2>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Project</th><th>What it holds</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>Machine</code></td><td>The emulated processor, memory and the C runtime of the original</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Imaging</code></td><td>Screens, pictures and fonts</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Acoustics</code></td><td>Sound drivers and synthesizers</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Foundation</code></td><td>Streams, compression, randomness, logging</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>GameModel</code></td><td>Game state and rules</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Core.Shared</code></td><td>Service interfaces, game memory, tables and texts</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Core.Engine</code></td><td>Drawing, sessions and platform services</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Core.Game</code></td><td>The game logic</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Core.UI</code></td><td>The game screens</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Core</code></td><td><code>GameEngine</code>, which puts the services together</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Desktop</code></td><td>The Avalonia window</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Tests</code></td><td>Tests, one folder per project</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="translation-fixes">Fixes of the translated code</h2>
  <p>Places where the C# translation behaved differently from the DOS game, and are fixed now:</p>
  <ul class="plain-list">
    <li>A starving city without a settler was always lost; now it loses a citizen.</li>
    <li>Computer research was announced as "... discover X from the Barbarians!".</li>
    <li>Wonders nobody had built or whose city was destroyed crashed the game.</li>
    <li>
      Civil war: technologies, the war between the two sides, splitting the cities, the capitals and units without a
      home city were handled wrongly or crashed.
    </li>
    <li>The military report crashed on a row of a player without contact.</li>
    <li>Computer players misjudged the strength of their own stacks.</li>
    <li>Tenths in the census and spaceship screens were wrong ("4.4" for 4.5).</li>
    <li>Spaceship parts were not drawn; a full part kind now says "No further ... required.".</li>
    <li>Only one city a turn could be hit by a disaster.</li>
    <li>The replay lost the high byte of the population.</li>
    <li>A threatening emissary named the wrong civilization.</li>
    <li>A new game cleared the ruined units of the wrong player.</li>
    <li>Continents and seas past the 63 ranked ones got garbage numbers.</li>
    <li>Demanding a tribute under peace gave 0 or 1 gold instead of the amount.</li>
    <li>A barbarian removed at the map edge got a garbage order.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Tests</h2>
  <p>
    The tests play turns of saved games without a window and compare the result with recordings of the first
    translation. A call journal records which functions the game called and with what, so a rewrite that changes the
    behaviour shows up as a difference. Without a window time does not pass, so the test clock ticks with the game's
    own memory reads and every run is the same.
  </p>

  <h2>History</h2>
  <p>
    The repository keeps every change with its date, but only the latest commits build: files that are no longer part
    of the project were removed from the whole history. To build and play, use the tip of <code>main</code>.
  </p>
</Layout>
